WEBVTT

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:24.869
All right. I wanna welcome you to today's special event, which is an event that I've basically done before. It's about Logos and Eros, but I had my little problems with it when I did it back beginning of the year, roughly half a year ago. I prepared for it. I prepared for it a long time. I did the event.

00:00:25.174 --> 00:00:38.211
The event was amazing because the people. That are coming there are amazing, but I wasn't satisfied. I was really not satisfied because I had not the feeling that I could get across what I wanted to get across.

00:00:39.125 --> 00:00:55.707
Those are really complicated concepts and I wanted to do it justice and I had a lot of ideas and I presented all of those ideas, but it did not make so much sense. It lacked a little bit of, like, structure and it lacked purpose.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:18.494
People still liked it a lot, but I had to say I did not reach the level that I wanted to reach. So I wanted to use the summer break to invest more time into it and to make it more approachable and more understandable what I tried to get across.

00:01:20.164 --> 00:01:39.855
Those are two very complicated concepts, and I did a lot of preparation for that already. Roughly a year ago, I did two offline workshops in Berlin. One was on feminine psychology and one was on masculine psychology, and Logos and Eros are very much associated with that.

00:01:40.448 --> 00:02:04.673
And what was special about those events was that, uh, on masculinity event was only with men in attendance and the On Femininity was only with women. And of course myself sitting in the background, listening, participating. And I learned there really a lot.

00:02:05.572 --> 00:02:23.218
And this was quite enlightening because I wanted to discuss there those concepts because I always had a little bit of a bad feeling, let's say, or let's say feeling for the limits of my own psychology. This is very much what Jung is about.

00:02:23.266 --> 00:02:46.165
It's about knowing one's limits and the worldview and abilities and capabilities and thoughts and feelings and all these different things. And he says men and women are different psychologically in certain aspects, even to a very great degree, and this is where he also doubted himself for certain concepts.

00:02:46.213 --> 00:03:07.051
So he checked that with female patients, with his wife, and other female persons in his life to make sure that he has a concept of the psyche, which is not only based from one standpoint, but rather from multiple standpoints to have something that is fundamental about the human experience.

00:03:07.677 --> 00:03:30.120
And I tried to do a little bit like this myself. And as I said, I did this a year ago and half a year ago, I kind of tried to present it and I did not reach the level that I wanted to reach. So this is now a redo. I am doing this basically all by myself, the usual location and with the usual preparation.

00:03:30.794 --> 00:03:53.562
But yeah, this is a little bit of a treat. During the summer break, and I want to get into this topic. And this is why this topic, uh, on Logos and Eros is with a subtitle of an investigation, because I want to take you through my struggles to define it because it's a little bit vague.

00:03:53.676 --> 00:04:03.480
It's pretty concrete, but at the same time, a little bit vague because it has multi facets. Jung has a lot of concepts. That are with multiple definitions.

00:04:04.009 --> 00:04:22.972
This is something that I talked about recently a lot, like the multiple definitions of the unconscious, the multiple definitions of shadow, and also Logos and Eros has really a lot of different definitions that kind of makes sense when you see them all together. So this is what I'm trying to do here, trying to bring them all together.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:48.275
And I already in the beginning said that this is very closely associated with masculinity and femininity, and in such also then with men and women, meaning not necessarily that masculinity means male and femininity means female, but I will get into that. This is what this event will be about.

00:04:49.014 --> 00:05:13.025
And I want to start first with something, let's say, very easy, very approachable, which is stereotypes and prejudices that they exist. There's certain things that are said about like men and women, the different genders, the different sexes, how they supposedly are in their behavior.

00:05:13.538 --> 00:05:35.212
For example, men don't talk about their feelings. Women overshare emotions. Men only think about sex. Women can't make up their mind. Men are aggressive when they solve problems. Women blindly follow trends and movements. And men don't have friends. So those are generalities.

00:05:36.624 --> 00:05:58.880
What I'm not saying is that this is universally true. This is also not what Jung is saying. But if there's one thing we have to always keep in mind when we talk about Jung, is he was always very interested about what people say and do. And he does not start from the standpoint of whether it's true or not, but there's a fact there.

00:05:59.041 --> 00:06:18.177
And the fact is that people are saying certain things. Kant, for example, the famous German philosopher, said he's not sure whether ghosts exist or not. Ghosts like spirits that haunt people. But it is kind of peculiar that people talk about ghosts.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:43.003
So there's something to it, even though that ghost might not be real, people still talk about it. Similar with God. There's a discussion whether God is real or not, but what is pretty clear, there is this discussion and it's in many, many different places. So Independent of whether there is a God or not a God, we are talking about God in the discussion whether he's real or not.

00:06:43.533 --> 00:07:03.375
And similar, Jung approaches a lot of things. He says human beings talk about it, they formalize it, they spend their time and energy on it. So there has to be something there, not necessarily that what explicitly is talked about, but there's something implicitly under it. So this is why it's also worth to look into stereotypes.

00:07:03.873 --> 00:07:27.677
And those stereotypes are very familiar, these prejudices that we have or might not have against the opposite sex. And what we kind of know— this is colloquial language— we have science studying whether there are differences between men and women and how those look like.

00:07:28.484 --> 00:07:49.478
And we see there's certain overlap. We see in behavioral studies where scientists record and analyze behavior and patterns that there are certain things that are associated very much whether one is of the respective sex.

00:07:50.248 --> 00:08:13.407
For example, there's a study from 2024 that says men can face distinctive loneliness risks especially reduced emotional intimacy and difficulty seeking support. Also another one: women score higher on agreeableness or related interpersonal warmth measures on average. That means compared to men. This is a study from 2010.

00:08:14.133 --> 00:08:37.072
And another study: women report stronger average interest in people-oriented social and artistic activities. So how do you do a study? How does a sound study look like? You take a huge group of people, you try there to record data, and you try also to get a lot of factors that could play into it.

00:08:37.619 --> 00:08:48.287
You try to get cultural factors, you try to get age, you try to get income, you try to take, for example, personality and a lot of other factors.

00:08:49.182 --> 00:09:03.994
Where you try to find something that is not determined by this factor, but rather by this one factor, where what the sex of the person is, to then point it down to like, oh yeah, very likely it's associated with that.

00:09:05.149 --> 00:09:20.238
And very interestingly, we see, for example, for the last point, this women reported stronger than average interest in people-oriented social and artistic activities. We can even break it down to countries.

00:09:20.578 --> 00:09:42.409
So we have countries in the world that have, let's say, more rights and freedom for women, how they can build their life, how they can pursue their interests. And what we see in those egalitarian societies is that women very, very much gravitate to people-oriented works.

00:09:43.214 --> 00:10:02.488
That means kind of interests and professions that are, for example, in the caring sector. So, and when you have women, for example, in the technical sector, this is very often in less egalitarian societies because there's very often less economic opportunity.

00:10:02.987 --> 00:10:23.624
So it seems like there is an interest that's even shown in very young stages of very small children. That there's a greater interest in women and girls towards people and social activities than men who are more interested in things. And there are multiple studies about that.

00:10:24.254 --> 00:10:45.291
And this helps a little bit from separating it out from like a stereotype, a prejudice, a thing being said to, oh, there's an observable, verifiable pattern. That can tell us something deep about the people, especially when we put them into an environment where they can really freely choose, where they have the options to pursue multiple paths.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:11:08.963
Because going back to the factors, when your only job and chance to get money is to become an engineer, a doctor, or an architect, this is then where all people will gravitate towards. But when you have multiple, multiple options that all provide a livelihood that creates a life that's worth living, then people can follow their interests.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:30.871
And this is what we can then see in, for example, Scandinavian countries who are very egalitarian in their opportunities that they provide to women. But it goes a little bit further that you can also go not only what is being said and what behavior is being noticed, It can also go into biology.

00:11:31.583 --> 00:11:54.899
And a very, very obvious example for men and women is that men produce sperm and cannot get pregnant, and women can get pregnant and carry out a pregnancy. So we have there biologically an asymmetry that is between men and women. This is kind of why it takes a man and a woman to make a new human being.

00:11:55.627 --> 00:12:01.745
And how many couples in the world work out? A man and a woman.

00:12:03.806 --> 00:12:28.371
And this goes down to even the cellular level that we know that medication, for example, needs to be tested separately on men and women because it's not enough to only study it on, only on men and suppose like, yeah, men and women. Are the same in the sense of that the effects of the drug will be the same.

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:49.095
And we know this, that there's special medication for men and women separately because the biology is working differently. And one really, let's say, great problem that you have is when you do medical studies, you don't do that on pregnant women.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:13:11.161
You don't do that, which leads to the situation that there, when a woman is pregnant, that she should really take no medication because it's not really clear what helps and helps not. Well, there is some, but it's really, really limited because there's not really studies about that. Studies involves large sample groups and it would be all pregnant women.

00:13:11.226 --> 00:13:24.979
And if something goes wrong, that would be horrible. It would be a catastrophe on all levels. And we had these cases in the past. Where there was medication that was really, really not fit for women who carried a child.

00:13:26.248 --> 00:13:49.367
And one of those examples is Contergan, which was a kind of a sleep help for pregnant women, and it caused huge deformities in the children that were subsequently born. So we have there biological difference also between men and women. We also see it like in bone density, we see it in upper body strength.

00:13:49.705 --> 00:14:08.776
There's also multiple studies about that. So why am I talking about that? Because it mirrors a little bit about what Jung talked about. Jung very much thinks in opposites.

00:14:08.922 --> 00:14:33.585
Like, this is when you have Certain staples in Jungian psychology, certain ideas that make an idea Jungian, it has to do with opposites. You have light and dark, you have up and down, you have male and female, you have the conscious and the unconscious. So he works very often in this contrast, and we see this contrast also in the world.

00:14:34.822 --> 00:14:57.331
It doesn't mean that men and women are fundamentally different. Completely biologically, completely socially, completely in their psychology, but they see notable differences. And this is supposed to help a little bit with the understanding of where Jung wants to go with masculinity and femininity.

00:14:57.379 --> 00:15:17.129
And it will become clearer when we go into the definitions of Jung there. So this is what we will be doing now. We're talking about masculinity and femininity. And for Jung, it's not only that, it's, it's an expression of fundamental cosmic laws.

00:15:17.726 --> 00:15:41.319
So of course, when we talk about behavior, the question is, okay, is this like naturally born behavior or is it something that's socially conditioned? And very often it's kind of Both. It's, it has an influence. Like you, you need culture to unfold what's biologically there, let's say. And certain things are not biologically determined. Not everything is biologically determined.

00:15:41.628 --> 00:15:57.880
You have both nurture and nature happening kind of at the same time. And Jung's idea and Jung's stance is that masculinity and femininity are something fundamental. But not only in this human psyche.

00:15:58.414 --> 00:16:16.580
They're not limited to that because we have male and females also in the animal kingdom with other animals. So it's not something that's uniquely human. And he goes even further saying that, oh, it's not only living things.

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:41.425
It's like you have it on many, many levels, this kind of interplay of something that human beings describe as masculine and feminine, because this is what we find in a lot of symbology and a lot of cultural things. That's certain behavior, certain patterns, certain imagery is described as masculine or feminine.

00:16:42.388 --> 00:16:58.573
And there we always have to be careful. That does not mean male and female. It does not mean males are only masculine, females are only feminine. This I will go into later, but for the moment, it's really, we are looking at these terms of masculinity and femininity and for Jung as a person of fundamental laws.

00:16:59.167 --> 00:17:12.992
And he uses there two terms, and the one is Logos and the other one is Eros. And Logos is a Greek word and it means to say, for example, something significant.

00:17:13.763 --> 00:17:35.659
And It's also that Logos is developed with a wide variety of senses, including like description, theory, explanation, reason, reasoning power, principle, and so on. This is a kind of definition from Routledge, which is a dictionary.

00:17:38.080 --> 00:18:00.288
Similarly, Eros. Also a Greek word, worth— Jung liked the Greeks— meaning desire. This is, for example, where eroticism comes from as a word. It comes from Eros, and Eros was also the goddess of love. Cupid is her name in English.

00:18:02.688 --> 00:18:27.296
And it means as much as to desire. To love. And this definition is from Wikipedia. So masculinity, femininity, something speech, something factual, descriptive, and so on. And the other one more interpersonal, desire, love, right?

00:18:31.087 --> 00:18:46.138
So we will now do an investigation into Carl Gustav Jung. Because there's a lot of places where he talks about Logos and Eros, and where I had a hard time to kind of find the commonalities to say like, oh, this is the one quote that really describes it.

00:18:46.605 --> 00:19:02.318
So I will go through a couple of quotes so that you at the end come out with a kind of feeling where these multiple definitions converge. So I start with the first quote. What have I to say about Logos?

00:19:02.896 --> 00:19:23.644
The nearest I can get is that it is a certain peculiar quality in a man's being which leads him to discriminate, to reason, to judge, to divide, to understand in a particular way. Man here, really, we have to understand as a generic masculine, so it applies to all human beings.

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:49.423
And one cannot understand all this without also thinking about of its antithesis, the equally intuitive concept of Eros, which would be then a principle of relatedness, seeing things together, gathering things together, establishing relations between things. So you already have it. You have this opposite. You have this opposite of Eros and Logos.

00:19:49.957 --> 00:20:14.267
To reason, to judge, to divide. This is then Logos, dividing. And Eros combining things, bringing things together. And further, Eros is interweaving, Logos is differentiating knowledge, clarifying light. Eros is relatedness, Logos is discrimination and detachment.

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:36.528
And further, the concept of Eros could be expressed in modern terms as psychic interrelatedness. And that of Logos as objective interest. And further, by Logos I meant discrimination, judgment, insight, and by Eros I meant the capacity to relate.

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:48.104
You're already noticing there's certain words that keep on repeating. Where Logos is ordering and insistence, Eros is dissolution and movement.

00:20:50.415 --> 00:21:13.812
Another quote: Eros brings things together, establishes dynamic relations between things, while the relations which Logos brings are perhaps analogies or logical conclusions. And another quote: The masculine point of view is the law. Again, masculine, not male.

00:21:14.828 --> 00:21:39.809
But the point of view of love is of course feminine. The one is Logos and the other Eros. One is law, the other one is love. And this seems very clear and understandable. Like, oh yeah, okay, it's attaching, detaching, isolating, combining. But then you stumble through these quotes and I read all the books.

00:21:40.873 --> 00:22:01.405
By Jung. So I read really a lot of things and I made a lot of marks and highlighted a lot of stuff where you think, ah, I got the concept now. I got the idea. And then he throws in some sentences or side sentences or quotes and paragraphs and you go like, huh, okay. I did not expect that. Okay. This goes, this goes way farther than I anticipated.

00:22:02.271 --> 00:22:22.645
And this is what these quotes are about, about that. This is not only about, yeah, a little bit of abstract things, but Every orientating function can function in Eros and everything can function in Logos. What does this mean? An orientating function has to do with the typology that Jung built.

00:22:22.709 --> 00:22:38.033
He says every person has a personality that's personal to that person. And he says there's four ways how to sense and process the world, which is thinking, feeling, sensing, and intuition.

00:22:39.582 --> 00:22:58.804
So what he's now saying is, oh yeah, thinking you can do in an Eros way or in a Logos way, in a combining way or separating way, attaching or detaching way. Intuition you can do like that, or sensation, also feeling. So it goes further into that.

00:22:59.353 --> 00:23:20.722
The functions are vehicles for the forces or influences or activities which emanate from those two principles, those two gods, Logos and Eros. And perhaps you can also understand that if there were no principles whatever outside of the functions, one could never hope to detach anything from the unconscious.

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:44.266
There must be something which helps one to detach a function, some principle outside which allows one to tear it away from the original lump of unconsciousness. So here we are going already in an applied way of seeing Logos and Eros, because it's like, oh yeah, yeah, I can combine things, I can separate things.

00:23:44.411 --> 00:24:06.016
But he says, for example, my feeling can be very much attached to the wrong things. Feeling for Jung describes how do I personally relate to something. Do I like something or don't I like something? This is the easiest way to understand it.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:27.542
The world is full with 10 billion things and you're encountering thousands and thousands of them every day, and most just pass by. There's not really— one does not feel drawn or repelled by them. But then there are certain things that one really likes, and then there are certain things what one really does not like. And Jung says That's not coincidence.

00:24:27.655 --> 00:24:51.848
It says something about you. There's something about, for you to explore about you being there emotionally involved for certain topics, for example, certain persons, certain activities. And you can totally be wrongly attached to a topic, a person, an activity, like an obsession, addiction.

00:24:52.698 --> 00:25:17.217
Or yeah, being completely engrossed with a person, positively or negatively, where someone has to detach their feeling from that. One is very intertangled with that. So someone has to divide and get a distance between oneself and the thing and one's feeling about it or that person. So.

00:25:18.114 --> 00:25:48.486
We starting already to see a kind of practical implementation and understanding how things could be viewed in this attaching and detaching way, such as our feeling or even our sensation or intuition or thinking, that we are in our thinking very much attached to a thing up to the point where we are very obsessive, then we have to remove ourselves.

00:25:48.840 --> 00:26:09.760
This is also what other people tell us, like, holy shit, can't you stop thinking about that? Can you? Are you only thinking about this and that? Or like, why don't you think about that? You should really think about that. You should attach yourself to this with your thought capacities. So going through all those quotes.

00:26:10.574 --> 00:26:37.451
And all those descriptions that Jung has, we can kind of make definitions and we can have these opposites that Jung very much plays with all the time of Logos and Eros, of masculinity and femininity, saying, oh, this separating and combining, detaching and attaching, isolating and relating, but also direct action and indirect action.

00:26:39.660 --> 00:27:03.168
Active and reactive, giving and receiving, differentiating and interweaving, static and dynamic, establishing and dissolving. This is already a lot of words how to describe it, right? Logos separating, detaching, isolating, direct action.

00:27:04.012 --> 00:27:21.606
Active, giving, differentiating, static, establishing. Eros, combining, attaching, relating, indirect action, reactive, receiving, interweaving, dynamic, dissolving.

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:45.899
So when you would look in the ways how Jung talks about these concepts and these ideas, these would be the terms you would be encountering, and already one gets a kind of feeling or intuition what they mean or how they could be applied. But at the same time, they're still pretty abstract.

00:27:47.381 --> 00:28:04.738
But there's a very good thing and way how they can become less abstract because Another big idea of Jung is that people communicate their psyche. People act out their psyche.

00:28:05.508 --> 00:28:26.068
When human beings create something, for example, art, culture, religion, fairy tales, spirituality, all these cultural achievements, they speak out of the psyche and And that way they are formed and shaped by the psyche and they correspond to the psyche.

00:28:26.614 --> 00:28:51.100
Popular things would not be popular if they would not speak to us, to our, let's say, individual psyche also. This is how things become popular. They come from a collective place, get expressed, and they talk to a huge amount of people because they're touching in a collective layer. And Jung called those expressions symbols.

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:58.173
And human beings really like symbols and creating symbols. And symbols have a meaning. They mean something.

00:28:58.286 --> 00:29:21.619
And he says they're not necessarily constructed through conscious effort by logical conclusion and all these different things, but rather they come from a deep-down creative part of the psyche where they kind of emerge And we try to understand them in hindsight. There's a huge discussion what something means.

00:29:22.998 --> 00:29:43.951
And what I want to get into now is really how to read symbols because it's, it's something that human beings don't do so much anymore. We kind of, we kind of, let's say, lost this access to our unconscious. And how our psyche operates.

00:29:44.464 --> 00:29:51.246
So Jung sees in the psyche different layers, and those different layers operate differently.

00:29:51.407 --> 00:30:15.127
Our conscious works different than our unconscious, and he sees there a kind of archaic layer in our psyche, how human beings are when they, for example, get born as children, but also how they were in the past where the, the psyche and consciousness was not so strongly formed through culture, education, rules, and all these different things.

00:30:15.464 --> 00:30:35.262
But human beings were more just living in the forest or in the desert in small tribes. So tribal, archaic psychology, where he says it's still like present in us, but we are not really connected to it so much. We try to connect to it.

00:30:35.808 --> 00:30:48.228
Through culture, dancing, going to the movies, reading something, music, and so on. It touches something. And when people create something, they write a story, they draw pictures.

00:30:51.229 --> 00:31:01.998
It is then also from that place where this psychology, where then the results are coming from, when they're not disturbed by the individual psyche.

00:31:03.522 --> 00:31:28.045
So to understand symbols, we have to put ourselves mentally in the shoes of human beings, how we lived thousands of years ago or tens of thousands of years ago, where we looked at the world very, very differently and how our psyche by default more looks to. Right now, our consciousness and our psyche is very much optimized, let's say.

00:31:28.784 --> 00:31:42.848
It's formed, trained like a muscle. And this is a very, let's say, rational muscle with rules, mechanistic to function in a very rule-based and mechanistic society.

00:31:45.750 --> 00:32:04.376
So we have to put ourselves back and understand that like symbols in this deep part in our psyche. Has very much to do with experience and how we initially experience things.

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:28.284
And what something is made of, for example, is secondary. And we very often start with, oh, how does something work? What does it consist of? This is a very scientific view, which is an incredible achievement that human beings were able to do that. And we're able to spread this knowledge.

00:32:28.332 --> 00:32:48.719
It's incredibly powerful to be able to think scientifically because it means we can live longer, we can live healthier, we can travel the world, we can do all those things. We can live in highly, highly complex societies and have a lot of things that we otherwise would not have.

00:32:48.815 --> 00:33:06.691
Because when you look at the Middle Ages and earlier, there were a lot of things that did not exist, like penicillin. Which are actually pretty good and that we would not want to miss and we would not have if we would not be able to think in that scientific way. But Jung says that's not the only way how the psyche works.

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:32.064
We also have a symbolic layer and it comes out in art and it also comes out during dreams. And this is why dreams are very hard to read, especially when you're trying to take them literally and not symbolically. And to interpret a symbol means how the experience is for us initially. We have to become very, very naive. We have to become very, very embodied.

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:57.953
We have to picture us in a situation. We have to remove us from abstract concepts. And for example, what we experience of water, right? What is water? The immediate experience, the bodily experience that we have there is wet, refreshing, or slippery. But another way to look at water is to say, oh, it's H2O.

00:33:58.294 --> 00:34:22.537
So you have two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, and then you have water. You even have more in there. You have minerals and you have bacteria and all these different things. But we only know this because somebody basically told it to us because of science, because of research and so on. But we don't really see that when we experience it. We don't see the atoms.

00:34:22.633 --> 00:34:45.919
We don't see the viruses in there. We have our experience. Wet, slippery, refreshing, life-giving, or even life-taking when we are drowning. So a lot of our symbology and how we talk about water and how it's represented in art or in our dreams, It's very much associated with, with that. For example, the unconscious is represented by water. Why?

00:34:46.705 --> 00:35:10.233
Because you can't look too deep into water. Things suddenly appear. Things can completely disappear. Nowhere to be found. It's a kind of barrier that's hard to traverse. That is also kind of dangerous, but, but also there can be very useful things. Like a fish suddenly can come out, out of nowhere, like a thought, like a feeling, like an idea can appear out of nowhere.

00:35:10.799 --> 00:35:33.806
This is why we have water very, very much associated with the unconscious part of the psyche. And this has to do with how we experience water. I see the water surface. I can't see very far below it, but things come up and I can go down there and I have no idea how deep it is. I don't know what there is. There could be a whole world there. So it's already filling imagination.

00:35:35.927 --> 00:35:59.234
And similarly, how people, for example, seen the Earth in the past was, of course, we see it from our point of view, from our perspective. We see, oh, the sun is rising and the sun is setting and the moon is moving and the planets and the stars and all these different things. And scientifically, of course, this isn't right.

00:36:01.313 --> 00:36:18.525
The sun is not setting, the sun is not rising. The Earth is rotating, but we, we don't feel this rotation. We don't see it. For us, everything seems normal and everything's stable, and it moves then around us. And we say, oh, this is the center.

00:36:18.589 --> 00:36:38.826
So this is where the world was really Earth-centered, the universe with the Earth in the center and everything else moving around it, like the sun and the planet and the stars and so on. Of course, now we say, oh, this is not true. Scientifically, that's not true. Like, we are moving around the sun, the Earth is rotating. This is why the sun is setting.

00:36:39.163 --> 00:37:03.139
This is why we have seasons and all these different effects that we have there. And we can explain it, like, how does it work? What is this made of? And all these different things. But still, when you want to do something romantic, you're gonna see the sunset. You're going to see the sunrise. You're not standing there and thinking, oh, the Earth is just rotating.

00:37:03.204 --> 00:37:22.460
